A||N||23||Monkeyman||Grape Ape needs help!||||9||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||03-15-2000||07:34 AM||||This is the 1st of many questions.  Here goes:  The battery in the Grape Ape is dead.  I replaced it with another almost dead battery this morning.  I hooked up a battery charger (had to get the trunk open at least--more on that later.).  The trunk opened and the headlights worked (I still can't believe both motors work!).  I turned the key and "POP", then nothing else.  The dash lights came on but the motor didn't crank (not surprised--more on that later, too).  Now I don't get power to anything.  No headlights, no trunk release (this is important as the lock cylinder has been screwdrivered), no dash lights.  What happened?  Could I have blown a fusible link?  Why would that happen?  I only had the charger on 10 amps.  I gotta get this thing straightened out soon.  ||||reg||9
Z||000001||BOILERMAKER||03-15-2000||08:12 AM||||From experience on other used and abused cars (others may have different advise) just take it in small (baby steps) increments.  One thing at a time or you will get very stressed out with all that has to be done.<P>I don't really have any advice on your electrical problem right now, but wanted to share with you that other advice.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||03-15-2000||11:29 AM||||Clean up all the battery cable ends and charge up a known battery that's sort of good. Put it in and try again. You may have to charge the battery overnight.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-15-2000||11:32 AM||||Check the fuses under the dash. You might have to pull each one out, to determine which, if any, are bad.<BR>Also, make sure the battery is able to hold a charge. If not, charging won't do a thing. It is possible the battery shorted out when you turned the key, I've seen it happen before. If you have a meter, check the voltage output on the battery too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||03-15-2000||12:59 PM||||Monkey, did you get a shop manual yet?  If not, I recommend Chiltons but the Haynes manuals are pretty good too.  I'd go with Fiero56's advice and start pulling fuses.  You can also buy a cheap device at an auto parts store(or even some gas stations) that allows you to check them without pulling them.  If it was my car, I'd probably start probing the engine bay for disconnected or damaged wiring. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000005||ROB MAC||03-15-2000||01:25 PM||||did you charge battery out of car? you should not use charger to start car.  running a car with a dead battery can cause problems also . ||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||03-15-2000||01:38 PM||||You need a voltmeter or test light to see where you are loosing power. If you don't have either and are on a budget you could probably make a test light with some wire and a tail light or similar bulb.<P>I would start at the battery. Check between + and -. Work your way out checking from various places that should be + (starter, fuse box, etc.) to various places that should be - (any ground). <B>Hopefully</B> the problem will become apparent at some point.<P>Good luck!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||mutfish||03-15-2000||01:42 PM||||Check the battery connection at the starter for rust/corrosion,or the solenoid internals...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Richard Parnell||03-15-2000||01:43 PM||||I had a similar problem on my Fiero.  I could turn on the lights, dash etc.. I checked that the battery had a charge, but still no start.  I tried turning on the ignition to no avail.  Then I opened the trunk and took another look at the battery, as I was looking closely I could hear a sizzling noise.  Battery was fried (don't know why)  But got a new, one car started right up.  What threw me off was that all the lights would come on so I thought for sure that would be enough to at least turn over the engine.  Try a good strong (or new) battery, it makes a difference.||||reg||14
Z||000009||william_thorniii||03-15-2000||04:28 PM||||Gentlemen,<P>These are all good suggestions.  I believe that MM in all his excitement has left out some very important information about the Grape Ape that would help us all in our diagnosis.  The Ape was purchased from an individual who had elected to park her for the last 9 months.  This individal does not know an EGR valve from an... ummmmm.... you know what I mean.  MM was told that the car would not stay running and was towed to its place of hibernation.  The Ape has not run since.  The seller quoted MM that the mechanics found a problem in the bottom end of the engine.  More specifically, the crank, bearings and or rods.  I think that this will really limit the accuracy of our speculations.  <P>MM,<P>You need to perform the seizure check that you did on the monkeymobile.  If the engine is not seized, then I would persue the electrical fix.  Please note that when attempting to listen to the engine for the first time that you should unplug the fuel pump fuse.  That way, you can turn it over for short periods of time (using the starter).  You don't want to crank it too much though because this will not only drain the battery, but also permanently damage the starter.  Listening should indicate if bottom end problems are present and If so should also hint at their severity.  I really don't think that it would be wise to try to actually start her up before damage is assessed.  As I have told you before, this engine might be salvageable at a low cost (for nearly nothing).  If that is the case, you don't want to blow it up just trying to hear it run IMO.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000010||ka4nkf||03-15-2000||05:13 PM||||Remember one thing when charging the battery, always disconnect the neg battery cable. If the charger happens to short out and charge too much with the cables still hooked up, you will fry the ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||03-15-2000||05:26 PM||||Another thing to remember when starting a motor that has sat for an extended period of time (or a new motor)is the cylindars and everything else (i.e., rockers, cam bearings, etc)is VERY dry. A good practice is to remove all the spark plugs and add a couple of "sqirts" of oil from a pump can (Add a piece of 1/4 tubing to the nozzle to reach the rear plug holes). <P>Once you've pre-lubed the cylindars, crank the motor until the oil pressure starts to register on the gauge. Then let it sit for a couple of minutes (to cool the starter) and crank it again. THEN, put the plugs back in and fire it up. This will go a long way to protect all those dry bearings and will save your starter.<P>Roy L.||||reg||3
Z||000012||Monkeyman||03-15-2000||11:44 PM||||Thanx everybody.  I don't think I made it clear as to exactly what happened.  1st thing I did was to hook the battery charger up to the old battery.  (The vent is missing.)  I set the charger on 10 volts.  I did this to activate the power trunk release as the lock cylinder is broken.  I got that to work fine.  Then I tried the headlights.  They came up slowly, but they did work.  I turned the ignition to "On" and got the correct dash lights.  I tried "Start" and nothing happened (battery was too dead, I guess.).  I took the old battery out of the Monkeymobile and put it in the Grape Ape (BTW, I need a new battery tray).  I put the charger on the Monkeymobiles battery (now in the Grape Ape), got in the car, turned the key and got a "Pop" or a loud "click" or something.  Now nothing works.  No headlights, no trunk selenoid, no dash lights.  What could have happened that would have caused this?  I can probably afford a new battery this weekend, but I'd hate to buy a new battery if some internal connection is bad (meaning that the new battery wouldn't do any good.).  I can't afford very much right now.  I'm starting to get a little frustrated.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||b_b_a_d||03-16-2000||02:36 AM||||0:  Keep your fingers crossed and pray for a blown fuse...<P>1:  Check all your fuses under the dash.<P>2:  Check voltage of battery with voltmeter.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> ...I'm thinkin... I'm thinkin...  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phaeton||03-16-2000||03:00 AM||||Only time a good battery ever went pop and quit on me was because of dirty terminals.  Did someone squeeze new terminals onto old cables?  Are the inside of the terminals coated with shiny white lead oxide?  Did the Fiero come with a gremlin?  If a sidepost, are the screw in's clean and scraped?  How about the bolt hole in the battery itself?  Was there distant thunder when the dash lights quit?  What do the terminals on the starter end look like? Am I going to do nothing but ask questions?||||reg||12
Z||000015||Monkeyman||03-16-2000||07:16 AM||||I checked the fuses under the dash.  All of them are fine.  Where else would fuses reside?  I know I need a new battery, but like I said, I'd hate to buy a new battery just to find out that it won't get juice to anything.  I'm on a tight budget.  Are there any fuses in the engine compartment?  Where?||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||03-16-2000||07:32 AM||||Clean all battery cable ends. Check the little red wire from the positive. Check all grounds. Borrow a good battery from the wife's car and see what happens. Did you check to see if the engine was seized like WT3 suggested? The pop was likely a dirty or bad wire connection.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||03-16-2000||10:32 AM||||Also, take both of your loose batteries up to your local parts house (Discount Auto, Autozone, Pep Boys, etc.).  They have devices that can test the batteries under load and tell you whether or not you have a bad battery or just a dead or low battery.  <P>If it turns out one is just low, then relax and let it charge overnight, and then try again the next day.  If you tried to start the engine with the battery charger on the batt. and the engine was frozen, its possible the pop you heard was the charger itself, because battery chargers werent meant for use as engine starters...you have to buy one of the engine starters for that.<P>While your waiting for the battery to charge, you might check the fusible links with a multimeter if you have one.  Several of the fusible links are in close proximity to the wiring harness and block just behind the battery (between battery and strut tower)<P>Hope this helps....||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||03-16-2000||12:15 PM||||There is a black "box" just behind the battery.  Is this where the fusible links would be?  What does a fusible link look like?  There are a few cylindrical pieces of plastic on some of the wires there.  Could these be the fusible links?  I have a small, cheap multimeter.  Do I just stick the probes through the insulation on either side of the link?<P>Sorry for all of the questions, but I obviously don't know squat about motors.||||reg||1
Z||000019||daved||03-16-2000||12:48 PM||||MM, Yep, those are they.  With battery disconnected and meter on "ohms", measure across the suspected link.  One probe on the end of the wire, and the other... I have used a needle or pin to puncture the insulation, on the other side of the link (that way almost no damage and no insulation is required afterwards). If not, take a sharp knife and nick the insulation to expose the bare copper (surest way).<BR>Put the probes together to test the meter first...should be "0" ohms, then try the link. should be low ohms, say 0.5 to just a few ohms.  If you get large numbers (say K or M ohms or infinity, then the link is blown.  <BR>You had better look for the problem before you replace it.  I suppose you could bypass the link with a large fuse (say 30amps) as a temporary fix...but never just short it out...otherwise a roasted Monkey could ensue!<BR>Thanks for your constant humour...many belly-laughs here...and good luck, D.||||reg||1
Z||000020||deceler8||03-16-2000||12:54 PM||||Fusible links usually look blown when they go bad...they melt and take the insulation with them, so if you're lucky, they will look like  they're bad.<P>Hope that's it.||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||03-16-2000||06:26 PM||||It's unlikely but posible a link went and didn't cook off. They nearly always cook. The fuse link on a wire isn't the plastic bead. It's the wire between the bead and the lug at the end.<P>If the thin + lead from the battery is gone nothing will work. Nearly all GM cars work like this. The thin bat ground can do this too, but it's not quite as common because there is more than one ground path. At least there is when all the other engine grounds are intact.||||reg||1
Z||000022||phillip||03-16-2000||06:56 PM||||i've had that problem twice over the 2 1/2 years i've had my fiero. the first time my battery cable chaffed and was shorting out.i guess if you have the same problem you would have noticed by now, but it acted like you describe.the second time my battery was just dead.it was in the car when i got it so maybe it's time was up. i replaced it and got the system checked at auto zone. no probles since. ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Monkeyman||03-16-2000||11:36 PM||||Thanx, guys.  I'll try to scope it out tomorrow.||||reg||1
